## Migration: .NET Framework → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ (`SYSLIB0006`)
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()`** — obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable for waking `WaitOne()`
3. **Race condition** — multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals; only one item dequeued per wake
4. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` not cancellable** — no way to cooperatively stop the loop

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while(true)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call interruption | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Timeout enforcement | `CancellationToken` + `Join` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new();
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Block until signaled OR cancelled
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            List<string> items;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                items = new List<string>(_queue);
                _queue.Clear();
            }

            foreach (var item in items)
            {
                if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
                    break;
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation only
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Changes Explained

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `ManualResetEvent` | `ManualResetEventSlim` | Lighter weight, `IDisposable` |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Unblocks on **either** new event or cancellation |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative; immediately unblocks `WaitAny` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Removed | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| Dequeue one item | Drain all items | Fixes lost-signal race condition |
| No cleanup | `IDisposable` | Disposes `CancellationTokenSource` and wait handle |

### Common Pitfalls

1. **"What if `Handle()` hangs?"** — Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle` and check it periodically. Cooperative cancellation only works if the code checks the token.
2. **Don't forget `Dispose`** — Leaking `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timer handles.
3. **`Stop()` won't force-kill** — If `Handle()` takes >5s, the thread stays alive. Design `Handle` to respect cancellation or accept bounded work.
4. **Thread vs Task** — You can optionally replace `Thread` with `Task.Run`, but the dedicated thread is fine for a long-lived consumer loop.

### Even Simpler: Use `BlockingCollection<T>`

If you don't need custom signaling, .NET provides this out of the box:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* expected on shutdown */ }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

`BlockingCollection<T>` eliminates manual locking, signaling, and the lost-signal bug entirely. `GetConsumingEnumerable` blocks until items are available and throws `OperationCanceledException` when the token fires — exactly the cooperative pattern .NET 8 expects.
